Chloe Smith is with us the work and  pension secretary representing the  government this morning great to see you  thanks for joining us why is the pound  in free fall  well I won't be able to comment on on  Market movements and of course lots of  factors go into any particular market  movement but what I can say is that I  think we've been right to set out the  growth package that we did on Friday and  my particular interest in that of course  is helping more people into work and I  see a lot of opportunity in what the  chances are set out to help people get  their way into more opportunity and from  their greater Prosperity the government  must be alarmed though to see the  reaction from the markets well as I say  I'm not going to be able to comment on  particular Market movements and there  are various factors that always go into  those but the government is absolutely  focused on delivering the growth package  as we set out with various ways that  will be helping both businesses and  households to move ahead to growth and  as I say to Greater opportunity and for  me in particular from the work and  pensions Department I want to then be  able to help more people into more good  and well-paid jobs so you're not at all  concerned of what the parent's doing I'm  extremely focused on how to go for  growth and that is what the chancellor  set out and I think was right to do so  okay  um will we bring back the triple lock on  pensions  as you know uh in the previous period we  used a double log because of some of the  particular abnormalities that happened  during the coffee period And as you also  then know the government has confirmed  that we'll be returning to the triple  law it's definitely going to happen so  I'm looking forward to supporting  pensioners in that way and of course  that also comes on top of the part for  the pensioners of the cost of living  package that we have been rolling out in  recent months and are you going to  increase that is it 150 pounds at the  moment isn't it are you going to  increase that further we're continuing  to roll out that package and as you may  know we are ensuring that payments for  disabled people go out this particular  week that's one of my focuses this week  and pensioners as part of that package  uh have received support and I think  overall that's been a comprehensive  package the reason I ask is because I  had mencap on the show last week and  they said that that 150 pound payment  should be doubled  well I hear that argument but what I  would say is that the package overall  has been a comprehensive one and in  particular let's take the example of of  a household that might include a  disabled person if that's if that was  main Cap's argument well of course what  we've seen there is that at least 1200  pounds worth of support so that that  includes support on energy bills 400  pounds we've got all those figures  not enough and that includes a special  one 150 pound payment for disabled  people now in addition to that I would  then say you should look at the energy  price guarantee that we have since I  know all those figures we know all of  those figures no well if I make a these  are really important and my view is all  about them I'm putting to you that  mencap have said that they want that  number done yes indeed and I'm putting  to you that actually disabled people do  quite understandably have concerns about  energy for example if somebody uses  equipment in their home that takes more  energy to keep charged an entirely  understandable scenario and that's why  the government has acted in several ways  on energy and that's is why that's a  really important point so that's a no to  make to you okay are you going to  reinstate the 20 pound Universal Credit  uplift no we've been very clear that  that was a temporary uplift to do with  the pandemic and as I say since then we  have been also very very clear and I  would say comprehensive in the way that  we've supported people through the cost  of living okay but you are going to lift  the ban on Bankers bonuses but you're  not going to bring back the Universal  Credit we did the calculation on the  back of an envelope and apparently  someone earning a million pounds will  save 55 220 pounds a year uh under  quaten's budget that's not the bankers  bonuses that's just bringing down the  top rates of tax that equates to  53 years of 20 pounds Universal uplift  should you not look at that again well  first of all okay I'm delighted that  your figures are on the back of an  envelope that's reassuring to your your  viewers well I wonder how quick these  things are done but the key point is  this we have put forward measures that  do cut tax across a range of points  you're just going to ignore those  figures I'm going to tell you why we're  doing it which is the most important  point of all because a growth package is  designed to lead to jobs and jobs and  opportunity and the point I will be  prioritizing as work and pension  secretary is that growth and jobs are  one of the best ways for people to get  greater prosperity and that is why I  support a package that includes a  cutting corporation tax includes a cut  in income tax at various points of the  spectrum and The Cutting National  Insurance for both individuals and  businesses because that will put money  back into the coffers of thousands of  businesses and thousands of individual  household budgets so that they have an  opportunity to grow and choose how to  spend that okay let me go back with to  those figures 55 000 pounds profit for  it however you want to describe it but a  millionaire will have another 55 220  pounds next year as a result of cutting  the top rate of tax that's 53 years of  Universal Credit and yet you're saying  you don't want to reinstate the  Universal Credit  getting another point on the issue of  where to locate Financial Services jobs  I'm not sure we want those jobs to be in  this country to be in London or indeed  to be in Norwich my city or to be in  Edinburgh or to be in Belfast rather  than to be in Paris or New York and that  is the point as the chance to explain  very very clearly in fact I'm asking you  that behind that policy and that is why  I think that is a good policy because it  brings jobs to this country and  encourages uh that to be here now those  jobs are in turn very very important for  uh claimants of Universal Credit who I  want to see go into more work better  work and a great career that is the  absolute focus of my department so you  ask me what I would like to see for  Universal Credit claimants I'd like to  see greater opportunities more jobs  better jobs and that's why this growth  package has been the right one okay so  people can't expect an uplift in  Universal Credit even though Bank  millionaires can get an extra 55 grand  next year  you've asked me the question in a few  ways several times and I think I've  given you a number of reasons why the  growth package is going to be good for  jobs let me take an example of the  National Insurance people who are  struggling to pay their bills really the  National Insurance point last week saves  uh saves 30 million households about 300  pounds that's significant if you don't  think that's significant then I'm sad to  hear that the point is that what people  need is opportunities and the growth  package creates those that National  Insurance cut for businesses for example  will mean that actually 900 000  businesses across the country have  around 10 000 pounds extra in their  budgets now 900 000 000 businesses that  could be a thousand in Norwich where I  live that could be a thousand where in  where you live I don't know where you  live okay but around our country in  every single place there will be  thousands of businesses who are able to  create more jobs from the Chancellor's  package and I think that's really  important only 81 000 people voted to  make list trust the next our present 