d in a prisoner swap with  Russia last week he said he was tortured  he certainly looks as though he's  suffered at the hands of his Russian  captors  um obviously against the Geneva  Convention what we're going to do about  it  well I'm pleased that John and others um  will be in a better position and I wish  them well personally of course there is  going to be a great deal more to do to  face down Putin's aggression and I think  the government is trust indeed while she  was foreign secretary and now prime  minister has been absolutely clear that  we cannot allow Putin's aggression to go  unchecked that is why we have such a  broad-based response to what has been  going on in Ukraine and elsewhere in our  relationships with Russia okay so we  will be speaking to Russia directly  about what should what should happen as  far as breaking the rules of the Geneva  Convention are concerned  I'm absolutely sure that the foreign  secretary will be taking that very very  seriously and communicate communicating  in the normal way with Russia and  echoing the government's overall stance  that Putin's aggression cannot go on and  cannot be unchecked okay um why do you  think the conservatives are doing so  badly in the polls at the moment  the conservatives will be in a position  to explain to the British people that we  have got growth going in the economy so  I want to go back to what the chancellor  announced last Friday and to what is for  me the absolute priority in my  department which is jobs and I know that  this is what really really matters to  people because that is of course one of  the best ways to improve household  income and find opportunity for yourself  and for your children but the markets  don't like it and the voters don't like  that so why do you think you're on the  right course that is what will matter  most to people and indeed to voters and  I have every confidence that the kind of  uh the kind of support that the  conservatives were delighted to have in  2019 will continue to follow Elizabeth  truss and be uh and be able to have a  conservative government in the years to  come but if the markets don't like it  and the voters don't like it why do you  think you're on the right path  because it is the correct path it is the  right path so everybody's everybody's  actually stepping apart from the  government it is the right path to help  people into jobs I don't think you could  argue on that particular Point okay well  I think I can look at the markets look  at what you're doing in the polls do you  not want people to be in jobs how would  you argue that point it's not um do I do  the questions you do the answers look  what the markets are doing and look what  the voters are saying yes and what we're  doing is going to make a difference in  the medium and long term we're going to  be able to help people into jobs you've  seen in the chancellor statement how we  are going to be going into uh the list  of projects that will make the most  difference to growth in this country as  well as then ways that will help  households and as well as the things  that will make a difference in the  medium to long term that is why I say we  are on the right path here because all  of those things will help people with  greater opportunity and put our country  back on the right track I fully stand by  that and I have every confidence that  people also will benefit from those jobs  as well okay and if I may on the ship  you may I'm being wrapped I'm being told  you have to go but do go on feel free  the sheer politics of it is that I don't  think labor have a Clear Vision or path  either well we'll wait and see uh thank  you for being so robust as always much  appreciated

